⚠️ Resolve Default Method Conflict in Java
When a class implements **multiple interfaces** that have `default` methods with the **same signature**,
Java cannot decide which one to use → **compile-time conflict**.

✅ Fix: Override the method in the class and resolve it yourself (call one/both, or write custom logic).

────────────────────────────────────────
✅ Example

```java
interface A {
    default void display() {
        System.out.println("Display from A");
    }
}

interface B {
    default void display() {
        System.out.println("Display from B");
    }
}

class MyClass implements A, B {
    @Override
    public void display() {
        A.super.display();                 // call A's default
        // B.super.display();              // or call B's default
        System.out.println("MyClass resolving display"); // custom logic
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        MyClass obj = new MyClass();
        obj.display();
    }
}
```

Output (with only `A.super.display()`):
```
Display from A
MyClass resolving display
```

────────────────────────────────────────
🔑 Notes
- `A.super.display()` = call the default method from interface `A`
- `InterfaceName.super.method()` is allowed **only inside** the implementing class (not from outside)
- If you don’t override → **compilation error**

────────────────────────────────────────
🧠 Related rules (interview favorites)

| Situation                                                         | Result                                      |
| ----------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------- |
| Two interfaces, both have same **abstract** method                | No conflict — class implements once         |
| Two interfaces, both have same **default** method                 | Conflict — class must override              |
| Class provides its own method + interface has default             | Class method wins                           |
| Sub-interface overrides default; class implements sub-interface   | More specific interface wins                |

────────────────────────────────────────
✅ Summary (Interview Answer)
"If two interfaces provide the same default method and a class implements both, Java forces the class to override that method to resolve ambiguity.
Inside the override you can call `A.super.method()` or `B.super.method()`, or provide fully custom logic."

🎤 Quick interview traps
1) Conflict is for **default vs default**, not for two abstract methods with same signature.
2) You **must** override — choosing is not automatic.
3) `A.super.display()` works only in the implementing class.
4) Class implementation always beats inherited interface defaults.
